## Solution

**Setup.** Let $h=\dfrac{b-a}{n}$. Reading off the displayed pattern, the sum inside the root is

$$S_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}f\!\left(a+\frac{k(b-a)}{n}\right)g\!\left(b-\frac{(k-1)(b-a)}{n}\right)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}f(a+kh)\,g\bigl(b-(k-1)h\bigr).$$

(For $k=1$: $f(a+h)g(b)$; for $k=n$: $f(b)\,g(a+h)$. This matches the three displayed terms.)

We assume $f$ and $g$ are continuous and **positive** on $[a,b]$ — the natural hypothesis ensuring every summand is positive and the $n$-th root is real and well defined.

**Bounding the summands.** Since $f$ and $g$ are continuous on the compact interval $[a,b]$, each attains a positive minimum and a finite maximum. Set

$$m_f=\min_{[a,b]}f>0,\quad M_f=\max_{[a,b]}f,\qquad m_g=\min_{[a,b]}g>0,\quad M_g=\max_{[a,b]}g.$$

For every $k$, both arguments $a+kh$ and $b-(k-1)h$ lie in $[a,b]$, so

$$m_f\,m_g \;\le\; f(a+kh)\,g\bigl(b-(k-1)h\bigr)\;\le\; M_f\,M_g.$$

Summing over $k=1,\dots,n$,

$$n\,m_f m_g \;\le\; S_n \;\le\; n\,M_f M_g.$$

**Taking $n$-th roots.** For any fixed constant $c>0$,

$$\bigl(n\,c\bigr)^{1/n}=n^{1/n}\,c^{1/n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}1\cdot 1=1,$$

because $n^{1/n}\to 1$ and $c^{1/n}\to 1$. Applying this to the lower bound $c=m_fm_g$ and the upper bound $c=M_fM_g$,

$$(n\,m_fm_g)^{1/n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}1,\qquad (n\,M_fM_g)^{1/n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}1.$$

By the squeeze theorem,

$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sqrt[n]{S_n}=1.$$

**Remark (independence from $f,g$).** The limit is independent of the particular positive continuous functions $f$ and $g$: only their boundedness and positivity away from $0$ matter. The factor $n^{1/n}\to 1$ swallows the linear growth $S_n=\Theta(n)$ coming from summing $n$ bounded terms, and every summand's $n$-th root also tends to $1$.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\sqrt[n]{\,f\!\left(a+\tfrac{b-a}{n}\right)g(b)+f\!\left(a+\tfrac{2(b-a)}{n}\right)g\!\left(b-\tfrac{b-a}{n}\right)+\cdots+f(b)\,g\!\left(a+\tfrac{b-a}{n}\right)\,}=1.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
